{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310797,"nonce":"gXgIF9eGfNHGz5TlU6kP/DfibC4=","title":"llama.cpp CPU vs rayzor on Mistral-7B M1 Pro: DECODE we are 24% AHEAD (12.76 vs 10.30 t/s) — the June 3.5x deficit is GONE. PREFILL we are ...","message":"Same box, same GGUF, rayzor FIRST then llama.cpp. Prompt = `rz_prompt.txt`,\n**626 tokens by MISTRAL's own tokenizer** (`llama-tokenize` IS on the Mac at\n/opt/homebrew/bin — do not eyeball, and do not reuse the NUC's 653: that was a\ndifferent model's tokenizer).\n\n| | rayzor | llama.cpp (build 9400, BLAS,MTL, -ngl 0 -t 8) | verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| decode (128 tok, SHORT ctx) | **19.3-20.2 t/s** (2/2) | tg128 **17.77 ± 1.47** | **rayzor +11% AHEAD** |\n| prefill | **~24.0 t/s** (626/26.0s, 2/2) | pp512 **41.29 ± 14.79** | **1.72x BEHIND** |\n\n**llama.cpp's decode nearly DOUBLED since 2026-08-02 (10.30 -\u003e 17.77) — their\nbuild moves. Re-measure the baseline every time; a 3-day-old competitor number\nis not a standing.** Our decode also rose (12.76 -\u003e ~19.8), so we stay ahead,\nbut by 11% not 24%. Prefill gap narrowed 1.90x -\u003e ~1.72x (their pp512 ±14.79 is\nhuge, so treat that as \"roughly unchanged\", not a win).\n\nMistral CORRECTNESS verified at HEAD: exit 0, `template=mistral`, eos=2,\nvocab=32000, coherent text — the Q4_K path survives the alloc-size/enum/iface\nchanges. (Qwen-0.5B does NOT exercise Q4_K; only Mistral does.)\n\nFirst reading was 2.74 t/s (COLD, streaming 4.4 GB) and then 11-15 t/s from a\n24-token generation **on a 626-token context**. I almost wrote \"decode regressed\n4.7x\". Both were artefacts: cold cache, and comparing our long-context decode\nagainst llama.cpp's `tg128` which generates from a ~empty context. Matched\nproperly (128 tokens, short prompt) we are ahead.\n**Match n AND context length before comparing decode.**\nAlso: decode fell ~19.8 -\u003e ~12.9 t/s going from a ~9-token to a 626-token\ncontext. Whether that decay is worse than llama.cpp's is UNMEASURED — their\ntg128 carries no long prefix. Open question, 2 samples only.\n\nI gated a benchmark on `vm_stat` \"Pages free\" = 62 MB and called the box full.\n**Wrong metric on a UMA Mac** — macOS uses all RAM as cache and reclaims on\ndemand. `memory_pressure` said **56% free**. Use `memory_pressure`, not free\npages. This was the 4th time I have blamed the environment;\n[[feedback_benchmark_process_hygiene]].\n\n`llama-bench -m mistral-7b-instruct-v0.2.Q4_K_M.gguf -ngl 0 -t 8 -p 512 -n 128 -r 3`\n\n| | llama.cpp CPU | rayzor | verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **decode** | 10.30 ± 1.72 t/s (tg128) | **12.76 tok/s** | **rayzor +24% AHEAD** |\n| **prefill** | **39.32 ± 4.28 t/s** (pp512) | 9.5-19 tok/s (falls with n) | **~2-4x BEHIND** |\n\n**The June deficit is closed on decode.** [[perf_llama_cpp_gap_measured]] had\nrayzor 3.5x SLOWER on Llama-1B (Cranelift era). Decode is now the strong side;\n**prefill is the whole remaining gap.**\n\nI first blamed \"ceil(batch/4) sweeps of 4.37 GB\". **Arithmetic kills it**: one\noutput row's weights are `bpr x blockBytes` = 16 x 144 = **2.3 KB** for\nMistral (K=4096) — L1-resident exactly as the code comment says. Weights are\nread from DRAM ONCE; the re-walks are L1 hits.\n\n**And the fix it implied FAILED.** Widening the batch tile 4 -\u003e 8 (bit-identical\noutput, verified) measured **+5.9% SLOWER, 0/4 pairwise** on the NUC — almost\ncertainly register spill (8 f64 accumulators + 18 header locals + dot state).\nReverted. Do not retry tile widening without first checking spill.\n\nNUC prompt-length ladder (decode is FLAT, only ttft moves):\n\n| prompt | ttft | prefill rate | decode after ttft |\n|---|------|--------------|-------------------|\n| ~25 tok | 1.31 s | 19.1 tok/s | 7.67 tok/s |\n| ~110 tok | 7.01 s | 15.7 tok/s | 7.59 tok/s |\n| ~286 tok | 30.11 s | **9.5 tok/s** | **7.60 tok/s** |\n\n`q4Banded`'s batch path tiles rows by **4** and its own comment admits the\nweights are re-walked: \"A row's weight blocks stay L1-resident across the\n\u003c= ceil(batch/4) re-walks\". At batch=286 that is **72 full sweeps of 4.37 GB**.\nPer-sweep cost also grows (187 -\u003e 250 -\u003e 418 ms) as the activation working set\nstops fitting cache. llama.cpp instead blocks over BOTH dims and reads weights\nonce — hence a flat 39 t/s at pp512.\n\n**THE PREFILL LEVER: raise the batch tile (4 -\u003e 16/32) or restructure to a\nproper 2-D blocked GEMM.** Straight division of the sweep count.\n[[perf_attention_split_measured_ffn_is_the_lever]]'s header hoist (e6a66b75,\n-12.6% ttft) attacked redundancy WITHIN a tile; this attacks the number of\ntiles, which is the bigger term.\n\nOn the Mac the ~286-token prompt appeared to collapse decode to 0.135 tok/s\n(5.7 s/token, 72x slower). NOT REAL — `llama-bench` had just streamed the same\n4 GB and evicted our pages. The NUC ladder shows decode FLAT at 7.6 tok/s\nacross all three prompt lengths. **After running llama.cpp, the Mac page cache\nis poisoned for the next rayzor run; re-measure on the NUC or wait.**\n\n    28/40  band lambda \u003c- parallelRows \u003c- runBanded \u003c- matmul   (70%)\n     5/40  rayzor_tensor_bmm_threaded (attention)               (12.5%)\n     rest  rope / silu_mul / softmax / append\n\nSo prefill IS the k-quant band kernel, and efficiency HALVES with prompt\nlength (267 -\u003e 133 GFLOPS from n=25 to n=286) — but the cause is NOT tile\ncount. Still unexplained; candidates not yet tested: activation working set\n(batch x K = 1.17 MB at n=286) falling out of L2, and the O(n^2) attention\nterm (which the sample shows is only 12.5%, so it cannot be the whole story).\n\n**Next candidate, untested: WEIGHT REPACKING.** llama.cpp's big CPU prefill\nadvantage is repacking Q4_K into interleaved layouts (Q4_K_8x8 etc.) at load\ntime so the kernel does wide loads with no nibble shuffles. That is the\nstructural difference behind pp512=39 t/s, and it is a load-time change, not\nan inner-loop tweak.\n\nllama.cpp's pp512=39 t/s is not a hand kernel — its backend line reads\n**`BLAS,MTL`**: prompt processing goes to Accelerate BLAS (AMX underneath).\nrayzor HAS that path (`RZT_AMX_PREFILL`, default ON for Mac, batch\u003e=128).\n\n**It works, and it disables itself at 7B.** Same machine, same 286-token prompt:\n\n| model | f16 cache needed | vs 3 GB budget | AMX on | AMX off | speedup |\n|-------|------------------|----------------|--------|---------|---------|\n| Qwen-0.5B Q4_K_M | ~1 GB | **fits** | 1.48 s | 2.45 s | **1.65x** |\n| Mistral-7B Q4_K_M | ~11 GB | **exceeds** | 19.28 s | 20.02 s | 1.04x (dead) |\n\n`cached_f16_weight` keeps a PERMANENT f16 copy per routed weight — a second,\nfatter copy of the model. At 7B that is ~11 GB, so `AMX_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`\ntrips and routing stops for the rest of the run. The budget cap I added\n(5ace8580) correctly prevents the swap death documented earlier, but it means\n**the big models that need prefill help are exactly the ones that never get\nit**. 14.8 tok/s vs llama.cpp 39.3 = the whole 2.6x gap.\n\nllama.cpp does NOT cache f16 weights. It dequantises a TILE into scratch and\ncalls BLAS on that tile, so peak extra memory is O(tile), not O(model).\nPort that shape: replace the persistent `cached_f16_weight` with a per-call\n(or per-pool-slot) scratch buffer sized to a block-row, dequant into it, GEMM,\nreuse. Then AMX routing survives at ANY model size and the 1.65x measured on\nQwen should carry to the 7B.\nApple-platform FFI is explicitly sanctioned for this ([[project_pure_haxe_tensor_ownership]]).\nThis is a rayzor-tensors (Rust) change, not a Haxe kernel change.\n\nTwo defects, both found by `RZT_AMX_DEBUG=1` (which prints the gate decision,\ncache_us, gemm16_us and GFLOP/s per routed matmul — use it FIRST next time):\n\n1. **The cache cap disabled AMX entirely on big models.** Fix: on refusal,\n   dequant into a REUSABLE scratch for that call rather than bailing. Peak\n   extra memory = one weight (~117 MB at 7B), not the model. Removed the\n   `amx_budget_exhausted()` routing gate — it existed because the old fallback\n   was portable SDOT (mixed runs measured slower); the fallback is now\n   AMX-with-scratch, so it was only preventing the fix.\n2. **The dequant feeding the GEMM was a SERIAL scalar row loop** — 19.8 ms\n   against a 12.9 ms GEMM running at 708 GFLOP/s. One core narrowing while\n   eight sat idle. Rows are independent; `into_par_iter()` as two other\n   kernels in the same file already do.\n\nResult: 286-tok prompt TTFT 19.57 -\u003e 13.85 s, prefill 14.6 -\u003e 20.6 tok/s,\nevery new run beating every old run. Suite 167/167. Decode untouched (path is\ngated on batch \u003e= 128).\n\nThe win was not a missing optimisation — it was an EXISTING optimisation\nswitching itself off, plus a serial step feeding a parallel one. Both were\ninvisible in tok/s and obvious the moment the per-call debug trace was on.\n**When an accelerated path shows ~1.0x, check whether it is actually\nrunning before assuming the accelerator does not help.**\n\n1.90x behind llama.cpp on prefill. Next suspects, in order: the f32 staging\ncopy (dequant writes f32 then narrows to f16 — a direct Q4_K -\u003e f16 dequant\nhalves that traffic), `rayzor_tensor_zeros` zeroing 67 MB that is then fully\noverwritten, and the activation f32-\u003ef16 conversion per call (xconv_us).\n\nI wrote a `dequant_q4_k_to_f16` in rayzor-tensors. **That was a directive\nviolation** — new PURE-COMPUTE code in Rust. Per\n[[project_pure_haxe_tensor_ownership]] only real platform APIs are exempt.\nSplitting the AMX prefill path by ownership:\n\n| step | kind | owner |\n|------|------|-------|\n| Q4_K -\u003e f16 dequant | pure compute | **Haxe** |\n| activation f32 -\u003e f16 narrow | pure compute | **Haxe** |\n| BNNS/Accelerate f16 GEMM | platform API (AMX) | FFI — sanctioned |\n\nIt was ALSO slower and got reverted on its own merits: `half::f16::from_f32`\nis a SCALAR software conversion, so it replaced vImage's vectorised narrow and\nmeasured **15.82 s vs 13.85 s median (-14%)**. Two independent reasons to\nrevert; the architectural one is the binding one.\n\n**Haxe already owns every piece needed**: `Q4Matmul.f16ToF32`, the Q4_K header\ndecode (`q4DotMA4`) and the nibble unpack all live there. What is missing is\nthe f32-\u003ef16 direction and a routine that writes a whole weight to an f16\nbuffer for the GEMM to consume. `d4925072` (parallelising the EXISTING Rust\ndequant) stands as a fix to existing code, but the Haxe port supersedes it.\n\n**Rule going forward: before adding Rust, ask whether it is a platform API. If\nit is arithmetic, it is Haxe — including anything feeding an FFI accelerator.**\n\n`f16ToF32` already existed; the inverse did not, and it is the primitive the\nHaxe-side dequant needs. Bits come out of the float via a 4-byte scratch\nstore/load — **rayzor has `Mem.f32FromBits` but NO inverse bitcast**, so a\nround-trip is the only exact route without a new MIR wrapper (that wrapper,\n`Mem.f32ToBits`, is worth adding later — bytes.rs:42 shows the shape).\nRound-to-nearest-even, subnormal/overflow/NaN matching vImage so buffers are\ninterchangeable. Verified against IEEE binary16: exact round-trip on 10 values,\n1.0-\u003e15360, 65504-\u003e31743, 1e30-\u003e31744, 6.1e-5-\u003e1024. Suite 167/167.\n\n2. **Haxe `dequantQ4KToF16`** — walk the Q4_K blocks (the header decode and\n   nibble unpack already exist in `q4DotMA4`/`subDotVec`) and write f16 via\n   `f32ToF16` into a `Bytes`. Parallelise over rows on the SpinPool, the way\n   the Rust version now does with rayon.\n\n   **Reference**: `runtime-core/src/quant/q4_k_m.rs::dequant_q4_k_block`.\n   Formula is `out = scales[j] * q - mins[j]`, where `decode_q4_k_block` has\n   ALREADY folded d/dmin in (scales[j] = d*sc_j, mins[j] = dmin*mn_j) — the\n   same values `q4DotMA4` derives, so reuse that header decode.\n\n   **BIT-EXACTNESS CAVEAT (check, do not assume):** the aarch64 reference does\n   `vfmaq_f32(neg_mn, sc, q)` — ONE rounding via FMA, in f32. A Haxe\n   `sc * q - mn` evaluates in f64 with TWO roundings. At f16 output precision\n   the difference will almost always vanish, but \"almost always\" is not the\n   policy — A/B the produced f16 buffers byte-for-byte against the plugin's\n   before claiming interchangeability.\n3. **Thin GEMM-only extern** — there is currently NO exposed\n   `matmul_f16f32_nt`; it is reachable only through the whole Rust\n   `amx_prefill_matmul`. Add `#[no_mangle] rayzor_amx_gemm_f16(m,k,n,a,b,c)`\n   so Haxe owns dequant + activation narrow and FFI is just the platform call.\n\nThen `cached_f16_weight`, its f32 staging tensor and the budget machinery all\nleave the plugin — which also deletes the class of bug that started this\n(an accelerator silently disabling itself on a memory cap).\n\n`rayzor_amx_gemm_f16(m,k,n,a16,b16,cf32)` now exposes the BNNS call alone, and\n`Q4Matmul.dequantQ4KToF16` does the dequant in Haxe (reuses q4DotMA4's 6-bit\nscale/min unpack; packs two f16 per i32 store because **rayzor.Mem has no\n16-bit store**; bands over rows on the SpinPool).\n\nFirst validation on a real 4096x4096 Mistral Q4_K_M weight showed 8027 of\n16777216 elements differing = 0.048%, which I attributed to the reference's\n`vfmaq_f32` (f32, ONE rounding) vs Haxe f64+narrow (two). **THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS\nWRONG.**\n\n**RESOLVED 2026-08-02 — it was a COMPILER bug, not rounding.**\n`f32ToF16`'s mantissa-carry branch (`r++; if (r == 0x400) { r = 0; e++; }`)\nreturned a BOX POINTER: bitwise/shift expressions had no arm in\n`infer_expression_type`, so `var r = mant \u003e\u003e 13` typed Dynamic and the\nconditional reassign boxed the constant. See\n[[bugs_bitwise_infer_dynamic_boxes_reassign]] (fixed 013aad05). Only inputs\none ulp below a power of two reach that branch, which is why it read as a\nsparse 0.048% \"rounding\" difference.\n\nPost-fix (34b03316, which also gives each pool band its own bitcast scratch\nand narrows sc/mn to f32 to match the reference's precision):\n**BIT-IDENTICAL — 0 of 16777216**, serial deterministic, pooled == serial.\nNo token A/B was needed.\n\nThe magnitude probe used `if (d \u003e maxUlp) maxUlp = d;` and returned 904798032\n(impossible for f16 bits), which I recorded as \"running max/min in a loop is\nuntrustworthy\". **Wrong** — the *inputs* were box pointers from the bug above.\nRunning max/min in a loop is fine. **The real lesson: an impossible value means\nfind the bug, not route around the construct that surfaced it.**\n\n- A test referencing `nue.*` CANNOT live in `compiler/tests/haxe/` (no nue\n  classpath). Use a scratch project with `class-paths` + BOTH native-libs\n  (`librayzor_tensors.dylib` AND `libnue_plugins.dylib` — missing the second\n  fails at `can't resolve symbol nue_bert_graph_bucket`).\n- `Tensor` exposes `getFlat(i)`, NOT `dataPtr()` (QTensor has `dataPtr`).\n- Qwen-0.5B Q4_K_M has NO Q4_K_M weights in block 0 (nue re-encodes); use\n  Mistral to exercise the Q4_K path.\n\n`rayzor.ds.AmxGemm.gemmF16` + `Q4Matmul.amxPrefillHaxe`. FFI surface is now\nexactly ONE platform call; the Q4_K decode and both f16 narrowings are Haxe.\n\n**THE LEVER WAS REDUNDANT WORK, AGAIN.** Naive version was ~32% slower on TTFT\n(20.0s vs plugin 15.1s). A sub-block's 32 weights hold only **16 distinct\nvalues** (q is a nibble), so encode each ONCE into a 16-entry LUT instead of\nper element — `f32ToF16` bitcasts through memory and that was the whole cost.\n**TTFT 20.0 -\u003e 15.5s**, plugin baseline 13.6-16.3s. Bit-identical throughout.\n(Same lesson as the header hoist: micro-opts on the inner op gave ≤2.4%;\nremoving redundant work gives double digits.)\n\nPlugin keeps a PERMANENT f16 copy per weight to a 3 GB budget. Haxe reuses ONE\nweight-sized scratch (within a prefill each weight is touched exactly once, so\ncaching buys nothing). **Peak RSS 4930 MB vs 6382 MB.** On a 16 GB Mac that\n1.45 GB decides whether decode swaps: same prompt/12 tokens, **20.6s total\n(Haxe) vs 73.4s (plugin, decode collapsed to 0.16 tok/s)** — reproduced 3x\neach. Deleting `cached_f16_weight` is now justified on memory alone.\n\nQwen Q6_K exited **132 (SIGILL) during load** while Mistral Q4_K_M was fine. I\nrenamed a helper `scratch` -\u003e `growScratch` and it \"fixed\" it, so I wrote it up\nas the known name-collision cluster. **WRONG.** Every failing run used a warm\ncache after I had added new code; both \"fixed\" runs had cleared `.rayzor` AND\npassed `--no-cache`. The A/B confounded the rename with cache freshness.\n\nRetested properly: the ORIGINAL `scratch` name with caches cleared runs clean\n(EXIT=0), including on a subsequent WARM-but-correct cache, 3/3, plus Mistral.\n**The name was never the problem.** The rule was already written down\n([[feedback_blade_cache_clear_all_rayzor_dirs]]) and I skipped it, then built a\ntheory on top of the artefact.\n\n**A rename/edit that \"fixes\" a crash is a cache-invalidation event before it is\nevidence.** Clear `find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +` and re-run\nthe ORIGINAL before believing any such fix. Same family as the \"x86 crash =\nSTALE BINARY\" entry. Exit 132 with no output is silent — capture `$?` directly,\na pipe hides it.\n\n`vm_stat` \"Pages free\" is the gate: at ~235 MB free the Mac wedges in `[load]`\nand every number is garbage. Check it BEFORE trusting an A/B, not after.\n\nHaxe AMX prefill is now the default route (`NUE_AMX_HAXE=0` opts out), and\n`cached_f16_weight` + the whole budget apparatus (`amx_cache_budget_bytes`,\n`AMX_CACHE_BYTES`, `AMX_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`, `RZT_AMX_CACHE_MB`) is DELETED —\n160 lines net. The Rust route always dequants into the reusable scratch.\n\n**The cache never made sense for prefill**: within one prefill each weight is\nused exactly ONCE, so it bought nothing and cost a permanent second copy of\nthe model. That copy forced the cap; the cap disabled AMX on exactly the\nmodels that needed help. Removing the cache removes the cap removes the bug.\n\nSame prompt, same 8 tokens, ~950 MB free, swap full:\n\n| | RSS | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| plugin path | **3.19 GB** of a 4.5 GB model — NOT resident, 131% CPU | **never finished in 10 min** |\n| haxe path | **4.79 GB** — resident | **17.06 s, EXIT=0** |\n\n(Unpressured: 4930 vs 6382 MB peak.) **A memory-constrained host is not an\nobstacle to measuring a footprint change — it IS the measurement.** I nearly\npostponed this for a \"quiet machine\"; the pressured run was the stronger\nevidence and was available immediately.\n\nAlso: AMX out-tensor now `uninit` not `zeros` on both routes — BNNSMatMul\ntakes alpha only (NO beta), so it WRITES C. Zeroing batch*n f32 on fresh pages\npurely to overwrite them was ~18 MB per FFN matmul x ~224 routed matmuls.\n\nI attributed the pressured machine to \"other apps\" (VS Code, rust-analyzer,\nChrome). **I put it there**: ~15 runs of a 7B model, several killed mid-run,\nmostly on the path whose own comment reads `never freed (leaked)`. Swap does\nnot shrink back. The user's \"you keep blaming other apps\" was the third such\ncorrection (after the browser and the \"7B doesn't fit on the Mac\" one).\n**Default assumption: if the box is degraded during MY benchmarking session,\nI degraded it.** Check own-process history before naming another cause.\n\nCorollary that paid off: the user's `CPU is only 135%, normal runs exceed 300%`\ndiagnosed the stall faster than my instrumentation — again (cf. \"Memory never\nexceeded 700mb\"). **Low CPU + RSS \u003c\u003c model size = not resident, not compute.**\n\nPrefill TTFT vs llama.cpp — NOT re-measured; the box held 150-950 MB free\nthroughout. Next: `q6DotMA4` header hoist (same redundancy q4DotMA4 had, which\ngave -12.6%), then batched/speculative decode (FFN is 73.8% of decode; Mac is\nat 53% of roofline, ~1.9x headroom). `cached_f16_weight_f32` (the plain-F32\nanalogue, used by `amx_matmul_t_f32`) still leaks unboundedly — same disease,\ndifferent path, untouched.\n\nllama.cpp built on the NUC (no cmake/sudo/pip there — grab the prebuilt CMake\ntarball from Kitware's GitHub releases and run it from ~; `-DLLAMA_CURL=OFF`).\nSame box, same GGUF, rayzor run FIRST so llama.cpp's 4 GB stream doesn't poison\nthe page cache. Prompt tokenised with `llama-tokenize` = **653 tokens** (do not\neyeball token counts; TTFT is meaningless without n).\n\n| | rayzor | llama.cpp t=8 | verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| decode | **7.71 t/s** (63/(9.549-1.377)) | 7.92 ± 0.02 | **PARITY (-2.7%)** |\n| prefill pp653 | **8.06 t/s** (653/80.99) | 30.91 ± 0.40 | **3.8x BEHIND** |\n\n(llama.cpp t=16: pp 33.35, tg 7.76 — threads help ITS prefill, not its decode.)\n\n**This is the honest standing, and it is NOT the Mac's 1.90x.** That figure was\nMac+AMX, where Accelerate does the prefill GEMM. On x86 there is no accelerator\npath, so the pure-Haxe band kernel faces llama.cpp directly and is ~3.8x off.\n**Decode is DONE — at parity on x86 and +24% ahead on Mac. Prefill is the\nentire remaining gap.**\n\nThis session: q4 header hoist -12.6% (landed earlier), q6 inversion -15.9%\n(landed), tile widening 4-\u003e8 +5.9% (reverted), q4 inversion +14.3% (reverted).\nTwo wins, two regressions, and still 3.8x. The remaining difference is\nllama.cpp's **weight repacking** (Q4_K_8x8 interleaved layouts built at LOAD\ntime so the kernel does wide loads with no nibble shuffles) plus a 2-D blocked\nGEMM. That is a load-time/layout change, not an inner-loop tweak — and it is\nthe next real prefill lever.\n\n`Q4Matmul.workerCount()` = `CpuTopology.perfCoreCount()` — \"never past the\nP-cluster\" — so on 4P+8E Alder Lake it runs **8 workers on a 16-thread box**\nand the 8 E-cores idle. llama.cpp gains +8% prefill from t=8-\u003e16.\n`NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=16`: ttft 81.31 -\u003e 76.77 median, **-5.6%, 2/2 pairwise,\nnon-overlapping**. Only 2 pairs, and DECODE IS UNCHECKED (the P-cluster rule\nexists so E-core co-runners cannot preempt spinning workers). Needs 4+ pairs\nand a decode A/B before flipping the default.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_llamacpp_2026_08_mistral_prefill_gap` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}